{% comment %}
  Product reviews (Figma: Reviews, 508:10348).

  The heading, and under it the shop's Judge.me review widget wearing the kit's
  clothes. The section owns the heading and the arrangement; everything with a
  review in it is Judge.me's app block, dropped into `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`
  by the merchant.

  ## Why the widget is a block and not markup here

  Judge.me renders the score, the rating breakdown, the review list, the sort
  control, the filter panel and the write-a-review form. None of it can be rebuilt
  in Liquid — the reviews are not in the theme — so the honest arrangement is the
  app block inside our layout, restyled. `"blocks": [{ "type": "@app" }]` is what
  lets it land here; the merchant adds "Review widget" inside this section rather
  than beside it.

  ## Half of this design is a setting, not a stylesheet

  Two choices in Judge.me's own dashboard do more for the file than any amount of
  CSS could, and both are set on this shop:

  - **Widget header → Theme: Standard.** This is what draws the 5→1 rating
    breakdown. The Minimal header has no breakdown at all — no element, and no
    per-rating count anywhere on the page or in the `judgeme.*` metafields — so on
    Minimal the file's bar chart is not styling that is missing, it is data that
    does not exist.
  - **Reviews section → Theme: Align.** This is what puts the reviewer in a column
    to the left of the review text, which is how the file draws a row. Before it,
    that column was a grid built with `display: contents` over three of Judge.me's
    wrappers, and it was the most fragile thing in this file.

  So if a row ever loses its left column, or the breakdown disappears, look at
  those two settings before looking here.

  ## The trap: Judge.me writes its palette inline

  Every `--jm-*` value is an inline style on `.jm-review-widget`, so a stylesheet
  rule for one loses no matter how specific it is. The colours are all set in the
  dashboard now and need no rule; what is left is the corner radius and the bar's
  thickness, whose settings the dashboard keeps behind its paid tier, and the width
  the app block writes onto itself. Those four are the only `!important` in this
  file, and inline styles are why.

  ## The other trap: their rules are a class plus a scope attribute

  Judge.me's own selectors are `.jm-thing[data-v-…]`, which is (0,2,0), and their
  stylesheet is a `<link>` the app injects after the theme's bundle. A rule of ours
  at (0,2,0) ties and then loses on order, so every rule below carries
  `.jm-review-widget` for weight rather than for scope.

  ## What is not styled here, and why

  The file draws a star row under the score. Judge.me's summary prints the number
  and the review count and has no stars element, and the score is not exposed on
  any attribute a rule could read, so there is nothing to style and nothing to
  build it from. The number is stated, and the breakdown beside it carries a star
  on every row.

  The file also draws "Was this helpful?" with a thumb either side. That is
  Judge.me's "Show review thumbs up and down", which their dashboard keeps behind
  the paid tier — a switch, not a stylesheet, so it is not worked around here.
{% endcomment %}

<div
  class="section section--page-width spacing-style color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}"
  style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
  data-testid="product-reviews-{{ section.id }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div
    class="product-reviews"
    style="
      --product-reviews-gap: {{ section.settings.gap_mobile }}px;
      --product-reviews-gap-desktop: {{ section.settings.gap }}px;
      {%- comment -%}
        The check glyph for the Verified tag. It is handed over as a custom
        property because a `{% stylesheet %}` block is static CSS — Liquid is not
        run inside one, so `asset_url` cannot be written where the rule needs it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      --product-reviews-verified-icon: url('{{ 'icon-ui-badge-check.svg' | asset_url }}');
    "
  >
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_ui-heading', id: 'heading' %}

    <div class="product-reviews__widget">
      {% content_for 'blocks' %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  .product-reviews {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--product-reviews-gap, 32px);
  }

  .product-reviews > * {
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  /* The file centres the heading and holds it to 510 so "Sweet words from" breaks
     before "Happy Customers" without a hard line break in the field. */
  .product-reviews .ui-heading {
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------- the widget */

  /*
   * Corner radius and bar thickness are inline on `.jm-review-widget` and their
   * settings sit behind Judge.me's paid tier, so these three need `!important` to
   * land. Every colour comes from the dashboard and needs no rule at all.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget {
    --jm-widget-border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm) !important;
    --jm-widget-border-radius-simplified: var(--ui-radius-sm) !important;
    --jm-review-widget-bar-chart-height: 4px !important;

    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
  }

  /*
   * The app block caps itself at its own max-width setting and pads 40 either
   * side, both written inline. The file draws the reviews in the page column with
   * the heading, so the cap comes off — otherwise the heading centres on 1280 and
   * the rows sit in 1200 of it, and nothing on the page lines up with anything
   * else.
   */
  .product-reviews .jdgm-review-widget {
    max-inline-size: none !important;
    margin-inline: 0 !important;
  }

  /* The section owns the space around the widget; theirs is a second helping of
     it and puts 96px between the heading and the score. */
  .product-reviews .jdgm-review-widget .jm-review-widget {
    padding-block: 0;
  }

  /* "Customer Reviews" is this section's heading, drawn twice otherwise. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__title {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------- the summary and the breakdown */

  /* The score, which the file sets at 48 in the display face, over the count. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-average-rating-display .jm-cluster {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-average-rating-display .jm-cluster > :first-child {
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 48px;
    /* -1.92px on the 48px cut, written as the ratio so it holds at any size. */
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-average-rating-display .jm-cluster > :last-child {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  /* A breakdown row is `5 ★ ▬▬▬ 64`, which is what the file draws. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-histogram .jm-stack {
    gap: 32px;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-histogram-row__rating,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-histogram-row__frequency {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-histogram-row__rating {
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-histogram-row__frequency {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  /* The track is the file's grey at 30%; the fill takes the widget's own colour. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-progress-bar {
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: rgb(var(--ui-color-gray-rgb) / 0.3);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-progress-bar__fill {
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------- the controls row */

  /* Buttons: the kit's primary and secondary, which the file draws at 12px Outfit
     Medium in caps with a 2px corner. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-button,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-action-buttons .jm-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Theirs fills the column it is in; the file draws it to its words plus 32
       either side. */
    inline-size: fit-content;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight, 500);
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-button--primary {
    padding: 14px 32px;
    border: 0;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-button--primary:hover {
    background-color: var(--ui-color-sky-blue);
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-button--secondary {
    padding: 14px 32px;
    border: var(--ui-hairline-width) solid var(--ui-color-primary);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white);
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-button--secondary:hover {
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  /*
   * Their button sits in a column inside the right-hand cluster, and that column
   * stretches, so ending the cluster is not enough. The column is a `.jm-stack`,
   * which is sometimes a block that spaces its children with margins and
   * sometimes a flex row — it differs between the header's themes — so all three
   * of the ways to end a line are set rather than guessing which one is live.
   *
   * Named down to the header's own row on purpose. The same cluster carries the
   * button in the empty state, where Judge.me centres it under "Be the first to
   * write a review" and the file has no opinion — ending the line there drags it
   * off the middle of the page.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__main-content .jm-action-buttons .jm-stack {
    justify-content: flex-end;
    align-items: flex-end;
    text-align: end;
  }

  /*
   * The Filters trigger and the star tags in its panel are the file's tab: 36
   * high, 16 in, outlined, filled when they are the one in force.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-custom-answer-filter,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-rating-filter__tag {
    block-size: 36px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-primary);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: transparent;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight, 500);
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-custom-answer-filter--active,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-rating-filter__tag--selected {
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  /* Their compact cut hides the trigger's label and leaves a bare icon square,
     which reads as nothing at all in a row of words. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-custom-answer-filter .jm-custom-answer-filter__text {
    display: inline;
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: inherit;
    font-weight: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-custom-answer-filter__icon svg {
    display: none;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-rating-filter__star {
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* Judge.me counts up from one star; the file counts down from five. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-filter-panel .jm-cluster:has(.jm-rating-filter__tag) {
    flex-direction: row-reverse;
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }

  /* Clearing the rating is the file's "All reviews" tab, so it is drawn as one.
     Judge.me hides it until something is applied, which is right — a tab that
     undoes nothing is noise. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-filter-panel__clear-btn {
    block-size: 36px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-primary);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: transparent;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight, 500);
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  /*
   * The panel the Filters tab opens. The file has no panel to draw, so it is built
   * from [ui-filter-panel]'s own recipe instead of invented: white, a hairline
   * drawn inset so the rows can run its full width, a 2px corner and 12 all round.
   * Their own bottom border goes — it is a rule across the page under a box that
   * has an outline of its own.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-filter-panel {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: 12px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--ui-color-border-gray);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  /* "Filters" and "Rating", in the kit panel's own heading style. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-filter-panel > .jm-cluster > .jm-text,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-filter-panel .jm-stack > .jm-text {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight-semibold, 600);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  /* Sort reads as the file's "Sort by: Most Relevant" — body type, no box. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-sort-filter {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    block-size: 36px;
  }

  /*
   * "Sort by:" is drawn in the file and the control carries only its own value.
   * The widget has the words already — as a visually hidden `<label>` — so this is
   * showing a name that is there rather than inventing one.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-sort-filter::before {
    content: 'Sort by:';
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  /*
   * The select is sized to its own value rather than to their fixed box, and the
   * chevron is theirs — absolutely placed over the select, which is what keeps a
   * click on the arrow opening the sort. So the room for it has to be paid for in
   * the select's own padding: 12 for the glyph and the 10 the file leaves before
   * it. Take that padding away and the value runs underneath the arrow.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-sort-filter .jm-sort-filter__select {
    /*
     * A select left to size itself takes the width of its widest option — here
     * "Highest rating" — so a shorter value like "Most recent" leaves a hole
     * before the arrow. `field-sizing` is what makes it hug the value instead.
     * Where it is not supported the select keeps the widest-option width, which
     * is the old behaviour and holds its place as the sort changes, so there is
     * nothing to fall back to.
     */
    field-sizing: content;
    inline-size: auto;
    padding-inline: 0 22px;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* Their leading glyph is a second arrow beside the chevron; the file draws one. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-sort-filter__icon--sort {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Theirs is inset 12 from the end, which leaves a hole after the arrow; the file
     ends the control on it. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-sort-filter .jm-sort-filter__icon--chevron {
    display: inline-flex;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- a review row */

  /* The file butts the rows together and rules a hairline between them, so the
     list's own 1.5em gap comes off and the row carries the 32px itself. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jdgm-review-list {
    gap: 0;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-item {
    position: relative;
    padding-block: 32px;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border-gray);
    border-block-end: 0;
  }

  /* The list draws a closing rule under the last row as well. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jdgm-review-list > :last-child .jm-review-item {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border-gray);
  }

  /*
   * Their star is a glyph in a font of their own, and each one is set inline to
   * 1.5em — so the row is sized from the parent rather than from the glyph, which
   * cannot be reached without `!important`. 9px here puts five of them at 77
   * against the file's 74. Scoped to a review: the breakdown's rows carry a single
   * star each and the file draws those at full size.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item .jm-star-rating {
    /* Inline-level, so it sits on a text baseline and carries a descender's worth
       of space under it — which lands on top of the 24 below and reads as 28. */
    display: block;
    font-size: 9px;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /*
   * The row's own spacings, which their stacks set from a scale and the file sets
   * by hand: 14 between the face and the name, 24 under the stars, 10 between the
   * title and the copy. A `.jm-stack` spaces with a top margin on each child after
   * the first rather than with `gap`, so that is what gets set — `gap` on it does
   * nothing.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item .jm-reviewer-info {
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item__content > * + * {
    margin-block-start: 24px;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-content > * + * {
    margin-block-start: 10px;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-reviewer-avatar {
    inline-size: 36px;
    block-size: 36px;
    min-inline-size: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-reviewer-avatar__initial,
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-reviewer-avatar__initial .jm-text {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-reviewer-info__name {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  /*
   * The file has no Verified pill — it draws "I recommend this product" in that
   * corner instead, which is an answer this shop's review form does not ask for.
   * The badge is the nearest thing that is true, so it stays, and it is built to
   * [ui-tag]'s recipe: white, a purple hairline drawn inset, a 2px corner, Outfit
   * in caps and a glyph 6 to the left of the words. The kit sizes that component
   * from custom properties rather than settings, which is the licence to run it
   * small here — beside a 16px name the tag's own 12/24 would shout.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-verified-buyer-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 3px 8px;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 var(--ui-hairline-width) var(--ui-color-primary);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white);
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  /*
   * Their badge is words only, and there is no way to put an element inside it, so
   * the glyph is a mask on a box the colour of the text — which is what keeps it in
   * step with the tag rather than needing a second colour of its own.
   */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-verified-buyer-badge::before {
    content: '';
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    inline-size: 12px;
    block-size: 12px;
    background-color: currentcolor;
    mask: var(--product-reviews-verified-icon) center / contain no-repeat;
  }

  /* Their words come in a pill of their own — a black hairline with its own
     padding — which inside the tag reads as a box drawn twice. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-verified-buyer-badge__text {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight, 500);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: inherit;
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-content__title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-content__body {
    max-inline-size: 709px;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-pagination-controls {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* The two lines the widget writes on a product nobody has reviewed yet: the
     invitation, and the heading over the other products' reviews it offers
     instead. The file draws neither, so they are set in the kit's own type rather
     than left in Judge.me's. */
  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-no-reviews-state__text {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-other-products-reviews-text {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------ desktop */

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .product-reviews {
      gap: var(--product-reviews-gap-desktop, 48px);
    }

    /*
     * The header's four parts, which Judge.me stacks and the file lays out in two
     * rows: the score beside the breakdown, centred as a pair; then the filters at
     * one edge of the page and Write a review at the other.
     *
     * The two `1fr` columns are what centres the pair — they take the slack
     * evenly — while row two spans across them and so reaches the page edges.
     * `display: contents` on their `__main-content` is what lets the breakdown and
     * the button land in different rows; it is one wrapper and it holds only those
     * two.
     *
     * Guarded on `:has(.jm-histogram)`: a product nobody has reviewed yet gets a
     * header with no breakdown and no filters, and that is better left in
     * Judge.me's own stack than fitted into a grid built for parts it has not got.
     */
    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header:has(.jm-histogram) {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr max-content max-content 1fr;
      column-gap: 64px;
      row-gap: 48px;
      align-items: center;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__summary-section {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__main-content {
      display: contents;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__star-rating-histogram {
      grid-column: 3;
      grid-row: 1;
      /* The file draws the group 381 wide, of which the bars take 308. */
      inline-size: 381px;
      max-inline-size: 100%;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header:has(.jm-histogram) .jm-action-buttons {
      grid-column: 3 / -1;
      grid-row: 2;
      justify-self: end;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header__filters-wrapper {
      grid-column: 1 / 3;
      grid-row: 2;
      justify-self: start;
    }

    /* Their empty-state box, which on a product with reviews is an empty div that
       would still take a row and both of its gaps. */
    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header > .jm-center:empty {
      display: none;
    }

    /*
     * The filter panel arrives in a `div` with no class of its own, dropped
     * straight into this grid — so with nothing placing it, it auto-fills the
     * first free cell, which is up beside the score. It comes out of flow instead
     * and hangs under the header, which is the row the Filters tab is in, left
     * edge to left edge with it. That is [ui-filter-dropdown]'s own arrangement:
     * the panel overlays what is under it rather than pushing the reviews down.
     *
     * Below 750 there is no grid and it is already the last thing in the header,
     * so it falls under the tab on its own and this is left alone.
     */
    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header:has(.jm-histogram) {
      position: relative;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-review-widget-header > div:has(.jm-filter-panel) {
      position: absolute;
      inset-block-start: calc(100% + 8px);
      inset-inline-start: 0;
      z-index: 2;
      /* The header is one of their stacks, which spaces its children with a top
         margin rather than a gap. Out of flow that margin still counts against
         `inset-block-start`, and the panel opens 24px lower than it was told to. */
      margin-block-start: 0;
    }
  }

  /*
   * The row's own two columns come from Judge.me's Align layout; what is left is
   * the width the file draws and the rule down the middle. 990 rather than the
   * theme's 750: the file gives the text 709px beside a 305px column, and at 750
   * the two together leave the body about 330px, which puts the title on three
   * lines and the name on two.
   */
  @media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item__body {
      gap: 20px;
    }

    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item__sidebar {
      flex: 0 0 305px;
      align-self: stretch;
      padding-inline-end: 20px;
      border-inline-end: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border-gray);
    }

    /*
     * The date is drawn against the top right, and the Align layout prints it at
     * the foot of the reviewer's column. Taking it out of flow is the only way
     * across, and it is safe here because the string is a date — it neither wraps
     * nor grows. It is the one bare `span` among that column's children, which is
     * what the selector leans on.
     */
    .product-reviews .jm-review-widget .jm-align-review-item__sidebar .jm-reviewer-info__details > span.jm-text {
      position: absolute;
      inset-block-start: 32px;
      inset-inline-end: 0;
      /* The stack's own top margin still counts against `inset-block-start` and
         would drop it 4px below the stars it lines up with. */
      margin-block-start: 0;
      font-size: 14px;
      line-height: 1.3;
      color: var(--ui-color-gray);
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

